WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.590
A heap gives us its smallest

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.070
entry in about log n steps.

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.230
So here is an idea for sorting

4
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.690
put everything into a heap

5
00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.810
and take the entries out one by one.

6
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.380
They come out in order.

7
00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.780
That works.

8
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.880
But it needs another structure

9
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.380
and extra memory.

10
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.530
Can we do it inside the array we were given?

11
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.970
We can.

12
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.509
That is heap sort.

13
00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.345
And it has a guarantee that

14
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.080
quicksort does not have.

15
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.370
In this video we'll see how heap sort works

16
00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:33.583
what it costs

17
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.142
and how it compares with merge

18
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.580
sort and quicksort.

19
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.290
Heap sort has two phases.

20
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.070
Phase one: turn the array into a heap

21
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.960
using the bottom-up method from last time.

22
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.692
This time it is a max-heap

23
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.475
every entry is larger than

24
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.260
or equal to its children

25
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.870
so the largest entry is at the root.

26
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.094
Phase two: swap the root with the last

27
00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.290
entry of the heap.

28
00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.710
The largest entry is now where it belongs

29
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.420
at the very end of the array.

30
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.653
Then shrink the heap by one

31
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.649
so the array's last cell is

32
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.980
no longer part of it.

33
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.610
The new root may be too small, so sink it.

34
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.050
Repeat.

35
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.150
Each round fixes one more cell at the back

36
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.270
and the heap in front gets shorter

37
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.960
until nothing is left.

38
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.573
Why a max-heap, and not a min-heap?

39
00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.116
Because we want the largest

40
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.646
entry to go to the end.

41
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.188
A min-heap would put the

42
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.770
smallest at the front, where the heap lives

43
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.640
and we would have to move everything.

44
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.554
And notice that no new array is needed.

45
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.093
The array holds the heap in front and

46
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.700
the sorted entries behind it.

47
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.990
The two share the same cells.

48
00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:50.480
Our entries: twenty, sixteen, nine, twelve

49
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.890
five, eight, three, fourteen, six, eleven.

50
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.570
Phase one gives this max-heap: twenty

51
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:08.850
sixteen, nine, fourteen, eleven, eight

52
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.150
three, twelve, six, five.

53
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.040
It took nine comparisons and two swaps.

54
00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.040
Round one.

55
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.380
Swap twenty with the last entry, five.

56
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.640
twenty is in place.

57
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.060
Now sink five: it swaps with sixteen

58
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.320
then with fourteen, then with twelve.

59
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.080
That is six more comparisons

60
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.830
and four more swaps counting the first one.

61
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.478
Round two puts sixteen at

62
00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.470
the end of the heap

63
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.750
and round three puts fourteen.

64
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.590
Each round costs a few comparisons

65
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.930
fewer and fewer

66
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.700
because the heap is shrinking.

67
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.800
At the end the array is sorted: three, five

68
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.200
six, eight, nine, eleven, twelve, fourteen

69
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.503
sixteen, twenty.

70
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.740
thirty-five comparisons and

71
00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.370
twenty-three swaps in all.

72
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.050
The cost.

73
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.650
Phase one is linear

74
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.420
at most two comparisons per entry.

75
00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.694
Phase two does n minus one rounds.

76
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.032
Each round sinks an entry through

77
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.360
at most log n levels

78
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.560
with two comparisons per level.

79
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.650
So at most about two n log n comparisons.

80
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.750
That is O of n log n, in the worst case

81
00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.010
whatever the input.

82
00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.830
No bad inputs.

83
00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.120
Quicksort cannot promise that.

84
00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:30.999
And the extra memory is a

85
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.680
handful of variables.

86
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.750
Merge sort needs a second array of size n.

87
00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.273
Let's compare

88
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.961
by counting comparisons on random

89
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.130
input of four thousand entries.

90
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.130
Merge sort needs about forty-three thousand.

91
00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.950
Quicksort, with the last entry as pivot

92
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.870
about fifty-five thousand.

93
00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.440
Heap sort, about eighty-three thousand.

94
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.596
The number n log n is about

95
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.017
forty-eight thousand.

96
00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.731
Heap sort pays about one

97
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.736
point seven times that

98
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.391
almost twice as many comparisons

99
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.790
as merge sort.

100
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.130
Why?

101
00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.370
Each time we sink an entry

102
00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.410
it came from the bottom of the heap

103
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.850
so it usually goes all the way back down.

104
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.250
We pay two comparisons per level

105
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.700
almost every time.

106
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.426
There is a second cost we cannot

107
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.040
see in these counts.

108
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.140
Heap sort jumps around the array

109
00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.088
from index j to two j plus one.

110
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.055
Merge sort and quicksort walk

111
00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.800
through memory in order

112
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.220
which computers like.

113
00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.290
In practice, heap sort is typically slower.

114
00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.420
Pause and predict.

115
00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.840
We sort three pairs by their first item

116
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.180
two with label a, two with label b

117
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.510
and one with label c.

118
00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.689
The two entries with key two should

119
00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:50.670
stay in their original order, a before b.

120
00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.580
Does heap sort keep it?

121
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:53.740
No.

122
00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.000
Heap sort returns one c, two b, two a.

123
00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.460
The two equal keys swapped places.

124
00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.760
A stable sort would give one c, two a

125
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:03.810
two b.

126
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.474
Heap sort is not stable.

127
00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.459
Swapping the root with the last entry

128
00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.950
moves entries across the array

129
00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.560
without regard to equal keys.

130
00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.390
To sum up.

131
00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.878
Heap sort builds a max-heap in place

132
00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.817
then repeatedly moves the largest entry

133
00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.420
to the end and shrinks the heap.

134
00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.940
It guarantees n log n, in place

135
00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.241
but it is not stable

136
00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.504
and it is usually slower than quicksort

137
00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.170
or merge sort in practice.

138
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.630
Some library sorts use it as a safety net.

139
00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.090
They start with quicksort

140
00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.678
and if the recursion goes too deep

141
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.132
they switch to heap sort

142
00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.800
to keep the guarantee.

143
00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.827
Next time: graphs

144
00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.743
where a priority queue will pick

145
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.590
the next place to explore.

146
00:05:49.217 --> 00:05:52.667
Let's write heap sort in Java, in place

147
00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:54.297
on an int array.

148
00:05:54.877 --> 00:05:58.607
The core is down-heap for a max-heap

149
00:05:58.607 --> 00:05:59.927
inside an array.

150
00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:03.367
The parameter n is the size of the heap

151
00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:04.561
not of the array.

152
00:06:04.627 --> 00:06:06.380
Cells at index n and above

153
00:06:06.447 --> 00:06:07.927
are the sorted part

154
00:06:07.927 --> 00:06:10.037
and down-heap never looks at them.

155
00:06:10.387 --> 00:06:12.265
It picks the larger child

156
00:06:12.337 --> 00:06:14.321
stops if that child is not

157
00:06:14.397 --> 00:06:15.737
larger than the entry

158
00:06:15.737 --> 00:06:18.547
and otherwise swaps and moves down.

159
00:06:18.897 --> 00:06:20.637
The counts array collects

160
00:06:20.707 --> 00:06:23.097
comparisons and swaps, so we can measure.

161
00:06:23.677 --> 00:06:26.647
Build max-heap is the bottom-up loop

162
00:06:26.727 --> 00:06:28.173
from the parent of the last

163
00:06:28.227 --> 00:06:31.807
index down to zero, down-heap each node

164
00:06:31.807 --> 00:06:34.397
with n equal to the whole array.

165
00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:36.607
Heap sort builds the heap

166
00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:38.234
and then runs a loop from the

167
00:06:38.287 --> 00:06:40.319
last index down to one.

168
00:06:40.407 --> 00:06:42.119
Each round swaps the root

169
00:06:42.187 --> 00:06:43.787
with the cell at end

170
00:06:43.787 --> 00:06:45.627
and then sinks the new root

171
00:06:45.627 --> 00:06:48.497
in a heap that now ends just before end.

172
00:06:48.847 --> 00:06:51.486
The array is sorted in place.

173
00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:53.259
The method returns the number

174
00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:55.167
of comparisons and swaps.

175
00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:57.397
Here is the real run.

176
00:06:57.397 --> 00:07:01.957
After building: twenty, sixteen, nine

177
00:07:01.957 --> 00:07:07.197
fourteen, eleven, eight, three, twelve, six

178
00:07:07.197 --> 00:07:08.117
five.

179
00:07:08.117 --> 00:07:10.947
nine comparisons and two swaps.

180
00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:13.807
Round one puts twenty at the end

181
00:07:13.807 --> 00:07:15.847
six comparisons and four swaps.

182
00:07:15.847 --> 00:07:18.947
Round two puts sixteen at the end

183
00:07:18.947 --> 00:07:21.177
five comparisons and three swaps.

184
00:07:21.527 --> 00:07:24.130
In the end, thirty-five comparisons and

185
00:07:24.197 --> 00:07:27.487
twenty-three swaps, and the array is sorted.

186
00:07:28.067 --> 00:07:29.737
Now let's compare with merge

187
00:07:29.797 --> 00:07:30.937
sort and quicksort

188
00:07:30.937 --> 00:07:32.697
on the same random arrays.

189
00:07:32.697 --> 00:07:34.817
For four thousand entries

190
00:07:34.817 --> 00:07:37.937
heap sort eighty-three thousand comparisons

191
00:07:37.937 --> 00:07:40.417
merge sort about forty-three thousand

192
00:07:40.417 --> 00:07:42.787
quicksort about fifty-four thousand.

193
00:07:43.137 --> 00:07:45.638
Heap sort is about one point

194
00:07:45.727 --> 00:07:47.687
seven times n log n.

195
00:07:47.687 --> 00:07:50.817
Still n log n, with a bigger constant.

196
00:07:51.397 --> 00:07:53.576
Heap sort is not stable.

197
00:07:53.667 --> 00:07:55.502
We sort three records by key

198
00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:58.207
with a generic version, taking a comparator.

199
00:07:58.207 --> 00:08:03.117
The result: key one c, key two b, key two a.

200
00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:06.297
Arrays dot sort on objects is stable

201
00:08:06.297 --> 00:08:10.387
and gives key one c, key two a, key two b.

202
00:08:10.967 --> 00:08:14.248
In practice you call Arrays dot sort.

203
00:08:14.337 --> 00:08:16.587
For objects it is a stable merge

204
00:08:16.657 --> 00:08:18.337
sort called Tim sort.

205
00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:21.237
For arrays of primitives like int

206
00:08:21.237 --> 00:08:22.957
it is a quicksort variant

207
00:08:22.957 --> 00:08:24.727
where stability does not matter.

208
00:08:25.077 --> 00:08:27.305
And Priority Queue is there when you

209
00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.567
only need the smallest entries

210
00:08:29.567 --> 00:08:31.407
or a running minimum

211
00:08:31.407 --> 00:08:32.917
without sorting everything.

212
00:08:33.497 --> 00:08:35.547
Two common mistakes.

213
00:08:35.547 --> 00:08:38.119
One: building a min-heap and expecting

214
00:08:38.187 --> 00:08:40.387
it to sort in place, ascending.

215
00:08:40.387 --> 00:08:41.947
It would sort descending

216
00:08:41.947 --> 00:08:43.537
or need a second array.

217
00:08:43.887 --> 00:08:46.937
Two: sinking with the wrong size.

218
00:08:46.937 --> 00:08:49.617
After the swap, the heap's size is end

219
00:08:49.617 --> 00:08:51.007
not end plus one.

220
00:08:51.587 --> 00:08:55.797
So in Java: max-heap, bottom-up build

221
00:08:55.797 --> 00:08:58.137
swap and sink with a shrinking size.

222
00:08:58.137 --> 00:09:01.227
And for real work, call Arrays dot sort.

